---
import BaseLayout from '../components/shells/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/atoms/Button.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../components/blocks/SectionHeader.astro';
import CategoryCard from '../components/blocks/CategoryCard.astro';
import CtaPlate from '../components/blocks/CtaPlate.astro';
import Media from '../components/blocks/Media.astro';
import { getCategories, toPageImage } from '../lib/catalogue';
import { closingBand } from '../lib/closing-band';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
import heroImage from '../assets/home/centipack-home-hero.avif';

/**
 * Home (§4.4).
 *
 * A stack of Sections, and nothing else. No spacing decision is made on this
 * page: `space` is the only rhythm control and there is no such thing as a
 * spacer div. Nothing below a Section sets a gutter, a max-width or a page
 * centering — most of which do not compile, deliberately.
 *
 * The three cards in section 3 are the CATEGORIES COLLECTION, through its
 * adapter, from the first commit — not a hardcoded trio that would have to be
 * migrated later (§5).
 *
 * Copy and structure are v1 (Figma uMHWj1JRHoqmY9uOQNfyL6, `Home v1.03`,
 * 1005:13599). Three things changed in that round and all three are
 * subtractions, which is worth saying because the page got better by losing
 * them:
 *
 *   - THE STAT BAND IS GONE. Four claims sat on a glass plate over the hero
 *     photograph. The design does not draw them — the frames that carried them
 *     were already `hidden` in the file, which open question 32 flagged at the
 *     time — and they are now gone from the page as well. `.hero-stats`,
 *     `.hero-stat` and the `--blur-overlay` token went with them rather than
 *     being left as CSS for markup nobody renders.
 *   - THE COLLAGE IS GONE. This page ended on its own closing block: five
 *     product shots scattered at Figma offsets around a heading, with its own
 *     copy and its own button. v1 ends it on the SAME `cta desktop` every other
 *     page ends on, so the band is now one component and one string
 *     (`src/lib/closing-band.ts`) across six page types instead of five plus a
 *     bespoke sixth.
 *   - THE HERO HAS ONE BUTTON. It had two; the design draws one secondary.
 *
 * The hero picture is also no longer full-bleed: v1 draws it CONTAINED at
 * 1344 x 560 with the page's own radius, which is what every other photographic
 * plate on the site does.
 */
const categories = await getCategories();
const cta = await closingBand();

/**
 * How wide a category card is DRAWN here, as `sizes`.
 *
 * Measured at the seven sweep widths, not estimated:
 *   320:288  360:328  390:358  430:396  768:230  1024:310  1440:440px
 *
 * Three-up from md; one per row below it, at roughly 92vw. Above the
 * container's own max-width the card stops growing, so the top tier is fixed px.
 */
const CARD_SIZES_3UP = '(min-width: 90rem) 440px, (min-width: 48rem) 32vw, 92vw';

/**
 * The hero picture. Normalized here the way the adapter normalizes a
 * collection's — same `{ src, width, height, alt }` shape — because Media takes
 * one thing and should not care that this one is a page asset rather than a
 * content field.
 */
/* THE PORTRAIT HERO LANDED, so this is a <picture> now rather than one wide
   file cropped to 1:1.25 by `.hero-media`. The crop was never the same picture:
   squeezing a 16:9 line-up into a tall frame kept the middle two products and
   cut the rest off both ends. The portrait shot is composed for the frame — and
   it is 1206x1509, which is exactly the 1:1.25 `.hero-media` asks for, so the
   art direction and the CSS agree instead of one overriding the other. */
import heroImageMobile from '../assets/home/centipack-home-hero-mobile.avif';
const heroPicture = await toPageImage({
  src: heroImage,
  mobile: heroImageMobile,
  /* RE-READ AGAINST THE PICTURE after the shot was swapped, which is the rule
     entry 46 set and the only way this string stays true. */
  alt:
    'The CentiPack range laid out together: two open white corrugated shippers branded CentiPack, one holding a kraft inner box and the other a white EPS foam cooler; three pouches standing side by side in navy, white and silver; a navy mailer box open on two containers and a navy drawer box slid open on glass vials; a navy pump bottle with its cap off beside an open jar of vials and its lid; and two navy gel packs marked "Puncture here", one standing against the shipper and one lying flat.',
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${SITE.name} — cold chain packaging for compounding pharmacies`}
  description={SITE.description}
>
  {/*
    1 — Text hero.

    `main`, NOT `narrow`, AND THE HEADLINE CARRIES ITS OWN MEASURE. The design
    sets this h1 in TWO lines inside a 912px text box, and `narrow` is exactly
    that box — 63rem less two gutters is 912px at 1440, which is the design's own
    number. It still came out in three lines, and the reason is not the layout:

      natural width of the line, measured at 64px   1876px
      two lines therefore need at least             938px each
      the narrow column gives                       912px

    Two lines are arithmetically impossible at 912px; the browser sets this
    string about 3.5% wider than Figma does at the same size and tracking.
    Measured by sweeping the cap: 980px → three lines, 1000px → two, at [888,
    988]. So the Section widens and the HEADLINE takes the measure instead —
    which is what `.measure` already does for body copy, one step up. The flex
    column centres it, so nothing here needs `mx-auto` (§3.1).
  */}
  <Section width="main" spaceTop="nav" spaceBottom="sm" class="text-center" reveal="hero">
    <div class="gap-xl flex flex-col items-center">
      <h1 class="hero-headline text-h1 text-primary">
        Cold chain packaging for high-volume compounding pharmacies
      </h1>

      {/* `text-large`, not `text-body`: the design sets this one paragraph at
          18px where the rest of the site's body copy is 16. It is the opening
          sentence of the site and `--text-large` is exactly the step that
          exists for it (see the token's note in global.css).

          680px is the column the design draws it in, and no token lands there —
          `measure` is 66ch of the current size, which at 18px overshoots by
          about 60px. An arbitrary value with its reason stated (§4.2). */}
      <p class="text-large text-secondary max-w-[42.5rem]">
        We manufacture complete packaging solutions for temperature-sensitive medications — from
        insulated shippers and refrigerants to custom boxes and pharmacy formats — backed by 20+
        years of combined cold-chain expertise and built for pharmacies that produce at scale.
      </p>

      {/* TWO BUTTONS, AND v1 DRAWS ONE. Added on instruction: the design's hero
          is a single 152px secondary, and the quote request lived only in the
          nav and in the closing band at the foot of the page. Recorded as
          AUDIT D10, because a control the design does not draw is a deviation
          whoever opens Figma next will otherwise file as a bug.

          `primary`, NOT `action`, and that is a real choice rather than a
          default. The nav's "Get a quote" is the tan `action` fill, so matching
          it here is the obvious move — and AUDIT F8 records that the tan
          measures 1.94:1 against the page ground where WCAG 1.4.11 asks 3.0,
          i.e. the button's EDGE is not perceivable. That is accepted for the
          nav; putting a second, larger instance of it on the hero would spread
          an accepted defect rather than contain it. The ink fill is the site's
          primary action, it passes, and the pairing with an outlined secondary
          beside it is what this hero had before v1.

          One line to reverse if the nav's tan is wanted instead: `variant`. */}
      <div class="gap-sm flex flex-wrap justify-center">
        <Button href={SITE.contactHref} variant="primary">
          Get a quote
        </Button>
        <Button href="/products" variant="secondary">Explore products</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/*
    2 — The hero picture, full-bleed.

    EDGE TO EDGE AND FLUSH TO THE BAND BELOW IT, on instruction. The design
    draws a 1344 x 656 plate inside the page gutter with 64px of ground under
    it (Figma 1327:24508, in a 1440 x 720 band); the ruling is the other way —
    the picture runs the full viewport width and the next section starts on its
    bottom edge. Recorded as AUDIT D8, which this hero has now entered, left
    and re-entered.

    THE RATIO IS STILL THE PLATE'S, 1344/656. Full-bleed changes how wide the
    box is, not the shape of the crop: at 1440 the picture comes out 703px tall
    rather than 656, showing slightly more of the same 756px file.

    `width="full"` keeps the Section wrapper — theme and vertical rhythm behave
    identically — and drops only the max-width and the gutter. No radius with
    it: there is no card edge to round when a picture meets the screen.

    `.hero-media` carries the ratio rather than the `ratio` prop, because Media
    writes that prop as an INLINE custom property and an art-directing class has
    to set `aspect-ratio` itself to beat it. See the note on `.hero-media`.

    NO `media-scrim`. A scrim exists to make type legible over a photograph, and
    the four statistics that used to sit on this one are gone.
  */}
  <Section width="full" space="none">
    <Media
      image={heroPicture}
      label="Home hero — the CentiPack range in one line-up"
      ratio="1344 / 656"
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
      sizes="100vw"
      class="hero-media"
    />
  </Section>

  {/* 3 — Three layers. The band the design sets on the cream ground. */}
  <Section space="lg" class="bg-subtle" reveal>
    <div class="gap-2xl flex flex-col">
      <SectionHeader heading="Three integrated layers available individually or as a complete solution. Built to perform." headingLevel="h2" size="h2" split>
        <Fragment slot="lede">
          Your products deserve more than packaging — they require a cold-chain system built to
          maintain thermal integrity from packout through delivery. We manufacture the complete
          solution: insulated shippers validated for 2–8°C performance, refrigerants, custom
          secondary packaging, and pharmacy formats
        </Fragment>
        <Button href="/products" variant="ghost" size="link" icon="arrow">Browse all products</Button>
      </SectionHeader>

      {/*
        CATALOGUE ORDER, left to right: cold chain, custom boxes, pharmacy
        formats. These were REVERSED here — nested order, inward-out, to match a
        lede that named them that way — and v1 draws them in the catalogue's own
        order under a lede that no longer names them at all. Same three records,
        one less thing to keep in sync.
      */}
      <ul class="list-bare gap-sm grid md:grid-cols-3">
        {
          categories.map((category) => (
            <li class="flex">
              <CategoryCard category={category} headingLevel={3} sizes={CARD_SIZES_3UP} class="w-full" />
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/*
    4 — Testimonial. One instance on one page, so page markup, not a block.

    THE RULES ARE BACK, ON INSTRUCTION. A bottom border on the section and left
    and right borders on the card, inset by one step from the page edge — the
    pair that makes the quote read as a plate set into the page rather than as
    another band. They came off when the current frame (1351:8662) turned out
    to draw neither; they are back because they are wanted, which is a
    different question from what the frame draws. Recorded as AUDIT D11.

    THE BAND IS 600px WITH ITS CONTENT CENTRED, which is where the air before
    the closing plate comes from. The design's card is `h-[600px] py-[96px]`
    centred, so the attribution ends about 141px above the next band; ours
    ended 97px above it, because the figure's padding was doing the whole job
    and there was nothing holding the band open. Measured before and after.
  */}
  <Section space="none" class="border-line border-b" reveal>
    {/* 96px of padding from md and a 600px floor, both the design's. The
        padding alone left the band 97px short of the plate; the floor is what
        makes the quote sit in the middle of its own band rather than at the
        bottom of a box its text happened to size. No floor on a phone — 600px
        of fixed height there is most of a screen of nothing. */}
    <figure class="border-line py-3xl md:py-4xl md:min-h-[37.5rem] px-lg gap-xl md:gap-3xl flex flex-col items-center justify-center border-x text-center">
      <svg class="text-brand w-icon" viewBox="0 0 44 28.66" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M22.17 28.66 33.8 0H44l-6.05 28.66zM0 28.66 11.53 0h10.3L15.8 28.66z"></path>
      </svg>
      {/* 794px is the measure the design sets for this one quote, and no token
          lands on it: `measure` is 66ch of the CURRENT size, which at 32px is
          over a thousand pixels. An arbitrary value with its reason stated, per
          §4.2 — the second display quote that wants this width makes it a
          token. */}
      <blockquote class="text-h3 text-primary max-w-[49.625rem]">
        <p>
          CentiPack has been great to work with. As our pharmacy has grown, they’ve been able to
          scale right along with us. They’re reliable, easy to work with, and their pricing has been
          some of the best we’ve seen.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      {/* v1's attribution, replacing "Director of Operations, high-volume
          compounding pharmacy". Shorter and less specific — which is the
          client's call about their own reference. */}
      <figcaption class="text-body text-secondary">Compounding pharmacy client</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </Section>

  {/*
    5 — The closing band, and it is the SAME ONE every other page ends on.

    This page used to end on a bespoke block: five product photographs scattered
    at the design's own offsets around a heading, with its own copy and its own
    button. v1 replaces it with the shared `cta desktop`, so the closing band is
    now one component reading one string across every page type that has one.
  */}
  {/* NO GROUND CLASS: the page ground is `bg/primary` and so is this band.
      It carried `bg-subtle` for one round, when the design had the whole page
      on `#f2eee8`; it has since moved back to only the card band being
      `bg/secondary`. Re-sampled off the frame with Figma's canvas excluded,
      which is the only way to read these reliably — see entry 69. */}
  {/* `space="md"` BOTH SIDES — one global step, 96px, on instruction. The
      design's `cta desktop` instance has no top padding and 64-96 below, which
      is what this carried; the ruling is that the closing band sits in the
      page's normal section rhythm like every other band. */}
  <Section space="md" reveal>
    <CtaPlate {...cta} />
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
